  "issue_number": 270,
  "issue_title": "train_cost: estimate_cmaes_cost under-prices a run when the worker pool lists a model more than once",
  "issue_body": "## Problem\n\n`estimate_cmaes_cost` promises (docstring) that `total_usd` prices all `worker_calls` at the blended per-call cost \"averaged over the pool.\" But when `worker_names` lists a model more than once, the total silently drops the repeated slots' calls:\n\n```python\n# src/trinity/train_cost.py:110-118\nper_call: dict[str, float] = {}\nfor name in names:\n    per_call[name] = ...            # dict keyed by name -> collapses duplicate names\ncalls_per_model = worker_calls / len(names)   # len(names) = number of SLOTS (incl. dups)\nper_model_usd = {name: round(calls_per_model * c, 4) for name, c in per_call.items()}  # iterates UNIQUE names\ntotal = round(sum(per_model_usd.values()), 2)\n```\n\n`per_call` (and `per_model_usd`) are keyed by model name, so a repeated name collapses to one entry \u2014 but `calls_per_model` still divides `worker_calls` by `len(names)` (the slot count). Each repeated slot's share of the calls is never priced, so `sum(per_model_usd)` no longer equals `worker_calls` priced at the pool blend.\n\n`scripts/train_cost_report.py` passes `--workers` (nargs=\"+\") straight through, so a repeated worker is a reachable CLI input (a natural way to express an over-weighted pool), and no test covers it.\n\n## Reproduce (pure Python, no torch/GPU/network)\n\n```python\nfrom trinity.train_cost import estimate_cmaes_cost\ne = estimate_cmaes_cost(population_size=10, m_cma=1, generations=1,\n    worker_names=['cheap','cheap','pricey'],\n    prices={'cheap':(0.1,0.2),'pricey':(3.0,6.0)},\n    avg_turns=3, avg_prompt_tokens=1_000_000, avg_completion_tokens=1_000_000)\n# worker_calls == 30 ; per-call: cheap=0.3, pricey=9.0\n# blended over the 3 slots = (0.3+0.3+9.0)/3 = 3.2 -> 30 * 3.2 = 96.00 (correct)\n# reported total_usd == 93.00  (one 'cheap' slot's 10 calls / $3 dropped)\n```\n\nA plain `['a','a']` reports exactly half the true cost. Because this feeds `below_receipt_floor` (the $15 receipt-gate check), an under-count can tell a contributor a plan clears the floor when it does not.\n\n## Fix\n\nSplit `worker_calls` across the pool **slots** (`worker_calls / len(names)` each) and fold each slot into its model, so a model listed twice keeps both slots' share. For a distinct pool this is identical to today's result; it only corrects the duplicated-name case to match the documented blend.",
